 "title": "Formula to Speak Better by MIT Professor",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator discusses an MIT OpenCourseWare video that shares a formula for speaking better. The formula breaks down public speaking or communication into three components: Knowledge, Practice, and Talent.",
 "key_points": [
  "K stands for Knowledge, which is the biggest contributor to how well you are able to speak.",
  "P stands for Practice, which is the second biggest contributor.",
  "T stands for Talent, which is the smallest element or contributor to how well you are able to speak.",
  "Having good knowledge about the subject matter helps you speak better.",
  "Natural talent is something some people can have, but it is not the biggest factor in how well you can communicate."
 ],
 "content": "The creator recommends watching the YouTube video \"How to Speak Better\" by an MIT professor on MIT OpenCourseWare. The professor presents a formula on the blackboard to explain how to speak better. The components are written as K, P, and T. K stands for Knowledge, P stands for Practice, and T stands for Talent. The creator explains that Knowledge is the biggest contributor, Practice is the second biggest contributor, and Talent is the smallest contributor. The creator notes that if you have good knowledge about a subject matter and practice a lot, you will be able to speak better, whereas natural talent is the smallest element of how well you are able to speak.",
